WEBVTT

00:00.160 --> 00:04.240
A coordinated fraud operation rarely begins with one obvious attack.

00:04.640 --> 00:06.320
It develops in stages.

00:06.720 --> 00:12.000
Each stage can create signals, but those signals may look harmless when teams review them separately.

00:12.320 --> 00:14.160
First comes reconnaissance.

00:14.480 --> 00:21.760
The group studies high-value products, promotion limits, delivery options, return policies, and support procedures.

00:22.160 --> 00:28.800
Repeated policy searches, scripted browsing, or clusters of sessions following the same path may reveal that research.

00:29.120 --> 00:39.840
Next, the group assembles resources: stolen credentials, synthetic identities, payment instruments, devices, proxies, phone numbers, and delivery addresses.

00:40.160 --> 00:42.160
One account may seem ordinary.

00:42.480 --> 00:46.160
Shared attributes across many accounts can reveal coordination.

00:46.480 --> 00:49.360
The operation then tests the merchant's controls.

00:49.680 --> 00:53.280
Small orders or low-risk account changes show what succeeds.

00:53.560 --> 00:59.360
When the group finds a working path, transaction value, velocity, or complexity increases.

00:59.680 --> 01:05.760
Declines followed by rapid changes in device, address, or payment method are especially useful signals.

01:06.080 --> 01:08.960
Successful activity must then be monetized.

01:09.280 --> 01:13.920
Goods may be resold, stored value transferred, or refunds redirected.

01:14.240 --> 01:21.920
Fraud teams should follow both the purchase and the destination of value, including beneficiaries that appear across otherwise unrelated cases.

01:22.240 --> 01:22.960
Finally,

01:23.120 --> 01:25.760
The group conceals its activity and adapts.

01:26.080 --> 01:32.640
It replaces blocked accounts, changes infrastructure, alters timing, or shifts products and payment methods.

01:32.960 --> 01:37.759
A rule that worked yesterday may push the operation into a different channel rather than stop it.

01:38.080 --> 01:43.039
The practical response is to connect signals across stages, systems, and time.

01:43.360 --> 01:47.440
Look beyond the isolated event to identify the operation behind it.
